pangolins are a really unique group of  mammals they are covered with these  scales that are made of keratin which is  the same material as human fingernails  and if those scales that are in high  demand and it's driving a lot of the  illegal trade that we see here at the  National Fish and Wildlife forensics  laboratory we're very much like a Police  Crime Lab in that we examine evidence in  a triangular fashion attempt to link  suspect that when the crime lab together  with that evidence we had assist  investigators at the state national  international level who investigate  violators while I'd the bars were the  only such full-service wildlife crime  lab in the world we were very much aware  that penguins have been killed for a  wide range of reasons these little  creatures have a hard time protecting  themselves there are defenses to roll up  into a ball which makes them easily the  poachers to collect Pangolin scales have  been used in traditional medicines and  different parts of the world for a long  time but it's just now that it's on this  larger commercial scale that's where we  see these species being frightened but  then we became aware of this interesting  story that you could grind up a Pangolin  scale and extract chemicals out there to  make the illicit drugs so there was this  rumor on online news resources that  Pangolin scales contain tramadol  tramadol is synthetic analgesic so it's  used to treat pain it's not known to be  synthesized in any natural system and  then there was on top of this even some  suggestion that the trade and pangolins  was driven in part by this demand for  for tramadol which was then being used  for the manufacture of methamphetamine  we weren't really sure to what extent  this kind of rumor was actually driving  the trade but it was something that we  could actually address with the data  that we had and actually look for it we  were asked to identify Pangolin scales  early on simply because they were being  seized as evidence  we actually probed the sample for  tramadol and we did that for a hundred  and four scales representing 104  different individuals of all species  alright so what we do is just go in and  try to find a spot that's minimally  invasive that people aren't going to  notice too much here and we don't need  very much so the dart can can obtain  profiles from really small pieces of  material so the dart is getting the full  chemical profile of that keratin it  gives us in a bit the entire spectrum of  what's in there and we can probe that  chemical profile to look for tramadol  specifically and that's what we did  it's telling you the size and the  intensity of the ions that are actually  in the sample  I came to this lab because I wanted to  do something that I thought could make a  difference we're just doing our small  part to help reduce illegal wildlife  trade we looked at all species and we  didn't find any evidence for tramadol  within our detection window so we're  confident that we didn't find tramadol  we've worked a lot of interesting cases  in which we've examined evidence on  rhino horn pills final horns themselves  sea turtle meat or seat or leather in  the glass eels wide range of wildlife  parts and products but I think the  penguin skills offered us something a  little different I'm happy to think that  our work is going to help stop the  killing of these poor creatures for a  myth for a story that simply isn't true  if that's the case we'll be happy indeed  [Music]
 

